<div>
  <img decoding="async" width="696" height="392" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/galaxy-768x432-1.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-post-image" alt="Banking System Uses Significantly More Energy Than Bitcoin, Research Shows" loading="lazy" style="float:left; margin:0 15px 15px 0;" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/galaxy-768x432-1.jpg 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-300x169.jpg 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-696x392.jpg 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-1068x601.jpg 1068w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-747x420.jpg 747w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-190x107.jpg 190w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-380x214.jpg 380w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy-760x428.jpg 760w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/galaxy.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 696px) 100vw, 696px" /></p> 
  
  <p>
    <strong>A new report shows that the banking system consumes more than twice the amount of energy that bitcoin does. The authors analyzed bitcoin’s energy usage and compared it to the banking and gold industries.</strong>
  </p>
  
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">
    Banking System Consumes Much More Energy Than Bitcoin
  </h2>
  
  <p>
    Galaxy Digital published a report last week entitled “On Bitcoin’s Energy Consumption: A Quantitative Approach to a Subjective Question.” The authors described, “This report took a quantitative approach, comparing Bitcoin’s energy usage to that of other industries.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Within the report was an energy consumption comparison between the banking system, gold, and bitcoin.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <img decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="size-full wp-image-459494 aligncenter" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/estimated-annual-energy-consumption.jpg" alt="" width="1666" height="800" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/estimated-annual-energy-consumption.jpg 1666w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-300x144.jpg 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-1024x492.jpg 1024w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-768x369.jpg 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-1536x738.jpg 1536w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-696x334.jpg 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-1392x668.jpg 1392w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-1068x513.jpg 1068w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/estimated-annual-energy-consumption-875x420.jpg 875w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1666px) 100vw, 1666px" />
  </p>
  
  <p>
    According to the report, the Bitcoin network consumes an estimated 113.89 TWh/yr in total. The estimate comprises miner demand, miner electricity consumption, pool electricity consumption, and node electricity consumption.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <img decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="size-full wp-image-459496 aligncenter" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/bitcoin.jpg" alt="" width="2284" height="788" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/bitcoin.jpg 2284w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-300x104.jpg 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1024x353.jpg 1024w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-768x265.jpg 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1536x530.jpg 1536w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-2048x707.jpg 2048w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-696x240.jpg 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1392x480.jpg 1392w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1068x368.jpg 1068w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1217x420.jpg 1217w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/bitcoin-1920x662.jpg 1920w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 2284px) 100vw, 2284px" />
  </p>
  
  <p>
    While the report estimates that the gold industry utilizes roughly 240.61 TWh/yr, it says that the banking system consumes the most energy.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    The authors noted that “The four key areas of electricity consumption associated within the broader banking system with enough data to establish acceptable estimates are banking data centers, bank branches, ATMs, and card network’s data centers.” They wrote:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      We estimate the banking system uses 263.72 TWh of energy each year.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p style="text-align: center;">
    <!-- growjs zone placement 31 -->
    
    <ins> <ins class="growjs-placement"></ins> </ins> <!-- end of growjs zone placement -->
  </p>
  
  <p>
    In conclusion, the report highlights bitcoin’s features that can offer financial freedom to people worldwide. Moreover, “The network can benefit the energy sector by creating perfect use cases for intermittent and excess energy. And the network will only scale further if network adoption warrants it.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Emphasizing that “Energy utilization is not necessarily a bad thing,” the authors noted that “Humans will continue to find new technologies that require more energy that challenge the status quo. Bitcoin is yet another example.” They then revisited the original question:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Is the Bitcoin network’s electricity consumption an acceptable use of energy? Our answer is definitive: yes.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p>
    <em><strong>What do you think about this research? Let us know in the comments section below.</strong></em>
  </p>
</div>
